{"results":[{"id":"6ae20c8c345d85127b06d6d0","name":"n0n Ransomware: Inside an AI-Assisted Extortion Operation","description":"In September 2026, an exposed staging server revealed the complete operational workspace of a n0n ransomware operator, including breach tools, encryptor source code, extortion infrastructure, and an AI coding agent environment with over $7,000 in paid LLM API usage. The investigation identified at least 23 victim organizations across 14 countries spanning multiple sectors. Every documented compromise relied exclusively on weak or stolen credentials, with no vulnerability exploitation observed. The operation combines data exfiltration with either encryption or complete data destruction, including backup deletion. The operator leveraged an AI coding agent configured with 154 models to generate hundreds of attack scripts, demonstrating human-directed operations augmented by artificial intelligence. By October 2026, n0n had listed 93 victims with combined ransom demands totaling $283.7 million, using credential stuffing against FortiGate SSL-VPN devices, exposed cloud keys, and compromised management portals a...","author_name":"AlienVault","modified":"2026-11-27T14:03:21.572000","created":"2026-10-28T14:54:04.165000","revision":2,"tlp":"white","public":1,"adversary":"N0n","indicators":[{"id":4550039679,"indicator":"beaconipv4185.157.161.93ntpsync.io","type":"hostname","created":"2026-10-28T14:54:04","content":"","title":"","description":"","expiration":null,"is_active":1,"role":null}],"tags":["backup destruction","n0n","credential stuffing","destructive extortion","ai-assisted attacks","llm coding agent","n0n ransomware","fortigate ssl-vpn","data exfiltration"],"targeted_countries":["United States of America","Argentina","Venezuela, Bolivarian Republic of"],"malware_families":["n0n"],"attack_ids":[],"references":["https:\/\/socradar.io\/blog\/n0n-ransomware-ai-assisted-extortion\/"],"industries":["Retail","Technology","Education","Telecommunications","Government","Healthcare","Transportation","Finance","Hospitality"],"extract_source":[],"more_indicators":false},{"id":"6ae20c8b7eea709e876863db","name":"PHP2Shell Weaponized After Japanese Real Estate Extortion","description":"An exposed staging server at 96.126.130.81 revealed an operator's extortion campaign targeting Japanese real estate websites between October 9-11, 2026, followed by PHP2Shell exploitation tooling. The operator, using the handle kkkkevin777, compromised one Japanese real estate services company and pivoted to nearly 20 related sites through shared hosting credentials. Stolen data included 16,061 customer records with plaintext passwords, income details, and addresses. The operator deployed ransom pages with an October 11 deadline and pressured victims by threatening to expose customer data. PHP2Shell tooling arrived on October 21, ten days after the extortion wave, indicating the operator was preparing new capabilities. Additionally, databases and emails from a Taiwanese company were exfiltrated. The server contained weaponized PHP2Shell reproducers, web shells, beacon logs, and scripts for lateral movement, though no confirmed PHP2Shell exploitation against live targets was documented.","author_name":"AlienVault","modified":"2026-11-27T14:03:21.572000","created":"2026-10-28T14:54:03.767000","revision":2,"tlp":"white","public":1,"adversary":"kkkkevin777","indicators":[{"id":4539484200,"indicator":"CVE-2026-109619","type":"CVE","created":"2026-10-28T14:54:04","content":"","title":"","description":"","expiration":null,"is_active":1,"role":null},{"id":4539484199,"indicator":"CVE-2026-109618","type":"CVE","created":"2026-10-28T14:54:04","content":"","title":"","description":"","expiration":null,"is_active":1,"role":null}],"tags":["kkkkevin777","cve-2026-109618","php2shell","shared hosting compromise","japanese real estate extortion","cve-2026-109619","beacon infrastructure","web shell deployment","customer data theft","credential reuse"],"targeted_countries":["Japan","Taiwan"],"malware_families":[],"attack_ids":["T1491.002","T1114","T1565.001","T1053.003","T1021.004","T1190","T1583.001","T1560","T1505.003","T1552.004","T1589.002","T1087","T1083","T1059.001","T1048","T1078","T1486","T1499","T1071.001","T1490"],"references":["https:\/\/socradar.io\/blog\/php2shell-weaponized-japanese-real-estate-extortion\/"],"industries":["Real Estate","Finance"],"extract_source":[],"more_indicators":false},{"id":"6ae205cefd1c69f6a9b3116d","name":"68 GB Stolen From a Philippine Government Agency's Overseas-Employment Databases, Exposed by the Operator's Own Open Directory","description":"An unidentified operator exfiltrated 68.837 GiB of database backups from a Philippine government agency managing overseas employment and migrant workers on 6 September 2026. The theft involved 27 SQL Server backups containing case-management systems including POEA and OFW variants. The operator inadvertently exposed their staging infrastructure via an open HTTP directory on a UpCloud VPS in Singapore, revealing a rebranded GodPotato privilege escalation tool (renamed Wrqsnx), PowerShell stagers, Impacket toolkit, and transfer logs. The payload leverages DCOM and RPC mechanisms to achieve SYSTEM-level access without exploiting vulnerabilities. Certificate analysis linked the compromised infrastructure to the Philippine Overseas Employment Administration portal farm. Initial access vectors and command-and-control channels remain unidentified, though tooling was delivered to agency portal hosts.","author_name":"AlienVault","modified":"2026-11-27T14:03:21.572000","created":"2026-10-28T14:25:18.105000","revision":2,"tlp":"white","public":1,"adversary":"","indicators":[{"id":4550040185,"indicator":"213-163-205-35.sg-sin1.upcloud.host","type":"hostname","created":"2026-10-28T14:25:18","content":"","title":"","description":"","expiration":null,"is_active":1,"role":null},{"id":4550040178,"indicator":"fcbfc74ddffad4789120c618bdf166c6c8aaca286fb2