late agents to compromise the host systems, involving profiling installations, stealing secrets including AI model API keys, downloading Poseidon malware, and installing unauthorized SSH keys. Two distinct threat clusters, designated AIA0009 and AIA0010, conduct synchronized waves of attacks across multiple panels. AIA0009 focuses on persistence and malware deployment, while AIA0010 emphasizes exploration and credential extraction. The campaigns appear highly automated and possibly agentic, utilizing AI to attack AI operations. Activities include container escape attempts, reconnaissance of host environments, and deployment of Poseidon RAT configured with Bunny.net CDN command and control infrastructure.","author_name":"AlienVault","modified":"2026-11-26T16:00:21.334000","created":"2026-10-27T16:12:32.060000","revision":2,"tlp":"white","public":1,"adversary":"","indicators":[{"id":4548212014,"indicator":"http:\/\/199.91.220.129:28471\/worker-health","type":"URL","created":"2026-10-27T16:12:32","content":"","title":"","description":"","expiration":null,"is_active":1,"role":null},{"id":4548150723,"indicator":"1cfee868e5e488bb862140cf288fce46a167aa5982900102356dac552fa4fa43","type":"FileHash-SHA256","created":"2026-10-27T16:12:32","content":"","title":"","description":"","expiration":null,"is_active":1,"role":null},{"id":4548212013,"indicator":"8fbdc86bb9a00a7a90ae2c10beead7461f7ccfb6","type":"FileHash-SHA1","created":"2026-10-27T16:12:32","content":"","title":"","description":"","expiration":null,"is_active":1,"role":null},{"id":4548212012,"indicator":"7945967abaf6b5977b40f75bf34d631c","type":"FileHash-MD5","created":"2026-10-27T16:12:32","content":"","title":"","description":"","expiration":null,"is_active":1,"role":null}],"tags":["ai harness compromise","credential theft","automated attacks","poseidon rat","cairn","poseidon","container escape","agentic operations","ssh key installation"],"targeted_countries":[],"malware_families":["Poseidon"],"attack_ids":["T1033","T1082","T1071","T1053","T1190","T1552","T1611","T1548","T1087","T1059","T1083","T1204","T1098","T1078","T1027","T1573","T1136","T1018","T1046","T1105"],"references":["https:\/\/norforge.com\/blog\/automated-subversion-attempted-takeover"],"industries":[],"extract_source":[],"more_indicators":false},{"id":"6ae0cc28c4875a80ccd6b804","name":"Camera Management Software Compromise Leads to Malware Deployment","description":"Attackers compromised camera management software (CMS.exe) to gain initial access to multiple organizational environments. Following compromise, adversaries deployed rs.win32.exe into user AppData directories and leveraged DLL sideloading by placing the legitimate Windows binary WpcMon.exe alongside malicious DLLs (IPHLPAPI.DLL and samcli.dll) in non-standard locations. These modified DLLs, based on zlib compression libraries, incorporated beaconing functionality to communicate with a command-and-control server at 144.172.116.37. Persistence was established through Run Key registry modifications. The camera management software, potentially linked to 3R Global and kvision.ai, lacked adequate security controls and logging capabilities. Organizations should treat camera systems and their management platforms as critical components of the enterprise attack surface, implementing proper access controls, monitoring unusual process chains, and restricting executable execution in user-writable paths.","author_name":"AlienVault","modified":"2026-11-26T16:00:21.334000","created":"2026-10-27T16:07:04.299000","revision":2,"tlp":"white","public":1,"adversary":"","indicators":[{"id":4549349029,"indicator":"8f8e88c54a9c56174974711eff99b4beb79536642fcca1902acdc3d4b1fd1035","type":"FileHash-SHA256","created":"2026-10-27T16:43:38","content":"","title":"","description":"","expiration":null,"is_active":1,"role":null},{"id":4549349028,"indicator":"70b2345302264c41504d59e6a63d6d908969740424b42b7373f10b4c8d74c87a","type":"FileHash-SHA256","created":"2026-10-27T16:43:38","con